(A) The current prompt fails to elicit "DEEPER" because it frames the decision as a surface classification first, priming models to force-fit changes into the four surface categories even when inappropriate. The instruction “If none of those four is clearly what changed, answer DEEPER” sets a high bar for negation, and models—especially at temperature 0—default to confident, formulaic surface attributions. The verb "carries" is underutilized as a diagnostic signal, and the confidence metric is uncalibrated because the model is not required to contrast surface vs. deep alternatives explicitly, leading to high but misleading confidence on deep items.

(B)  
```text
You evaluate a change between two document versions. First, name the grammar verb the change enacts: attest (adds a claim), authorize (grants/denies standing), replace (substitutes content), withdraw (removes a prior contribution), recant (declares a prior contribution wrong), carries (a change of one kind arriving dressed as another).
Then, determine: is the change BEST explained as one of the SURFACE FOUR (Facts, Rules, Manner, Identity) or does it fundamentally involve a DEEP kind (Priorities, Confidence, Circumstances, Process, Model, Structure, Premises, Record)?
To decide: ask whether the change alters a surface assertion or instead shifts the underlying framework, standard, or structure. If the latter, output "DEEPER" even if a surface label seems applicable. A change that replaces a fact but also shifts the model of reasoning is DEEPER. A change in tone or phrasing without deeper impact is Manner. If in doubt, prefer DEEPER.
Now analyze:
<ITEM>
Reply STRICT JSON: {"surface": "<Facts|Rules|Manner|Identity|DEEPER>", "verb": "<one verb>", "deep_candidate": "<one of the eight deep kinds or 'none'>", "rationale": "<one sentence explaining why the change is not purely surface if DEEPER, or why surface suffices otherwise>"}
```

